frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Padouk Db5 over a closed-open tube of Part II's measured A4 radius, its stop set to sit the tube on this bar; its G_LT/E_L padouk's (Bremaud et al. 2010), its undercut solved again on it, and the broadband observer as its receiver; everything else as the rendered Db5 card.
name	padouk-db5-tubetuned-boxwood-f4
source	Bar: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544: Bar No. 5 (G4, 397 Hz), xylophone CONCORDE X4001, padouk, L 29.3 cm, b 3.7 cm, h 1.9 cm edge; E, density, gamma_B through the packs named below. Eta 3.86e-7 s is read from Fig. 4 of the same paper (alpha = 6.11 + 7.62e-6 f^2, Eq. 19), 4.22 times Table I's 9.16e-8; why the two differ is not known and they may not be the same bar. Ratios: G_LT/E_L of African padouk heartwood, 1/(a' + 2 nu) = 0.103, with a' = 8.8 from Bremaud et al., Identification of anisotropic vibrational properties of Padauk wood with interlocked grain, Wood Sci. Technol. 44(3), 355-367 (2010), Table 1 and Eq. 2, and nu the paper's "about 0.46", not a measurement; the other seven ratios are the USDA Wood Handbook FPL-GTR-282's Honduras mahogany, a stand-in species, with no padouk value in them. Tube: V. Doutaut, D. Matignon and A. Chaigne, Numerical simulations of xylophones. II, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 104(3), 1633-1647 (1998): the radius of its A4 resonator, closed at one end, a_T = 1.85 cm (p.1641; measured in Fig. 9, p.1643). Gap: W. E. Flynt, The Construction and Tuning of Vibrating Bars, AMICA Bulletin, December 1972 and March 1973, revised for Mechanical Music Digest, January 2009: "About 1/4 to 1/2 inch spacing is common"; the 1/2 in end.
note	NOT in the source: the tube's stop. No source prints a stop for this tube, so this card sets it as a maker would, at 142.209 mm, solved once on the passive column with the bar's reflection in it for the cut before these ratios; on this bar it leaves the tube's first resonance -0.016 c from this bar's own computed f1 of 554.365 Hz, from this card's modes, under the 0.1 c that would set it again, and the law here reads 142.208 mm; no recording enters. NOT in the source either: the undercut's shape on this note (solved here in the engine on this card's ratios and 56 x 8 x 4 mesh, a circular arc, reach 72.213 mm, middle 13.434 mm, so f1 sits at equal temperament and f2 = 3 f1; the printed bar itself is tuned about 1:4 at 397 Hz, Table II; the 1:3 target is from xylophone tuning practice in print, not from the recording). The tube's radius is Part II's measured A4 resonator's, 18.5 mm, read as the inside radius and carried to this note, 4 semitones above A4; Part II names no instrument for that tube (its G4 comparisons are on the CONCORDE X4001 of Part I) and prints no wall or stopper. The gap is 12.7 mm below the plane of the uncut lower face, so the wood at the bar's middle is a further 5.6 mm from the mouth: the 1/2 in end of Flynt's printed 1/4 to 1/2 in, the end nearer Part II's "a few centimeters only", chosen before any render. The tube is a closed-open column of four odd modes, every one under this card's band that the open-end law takes (ka under 1.5), coupled through the bar's own boundary elements at each tube mode's frequency; its wall doesn't scatter, the mouth's sound doesn't reflect off the bar on its way out, and there are no neighbouring bars or frame. Its mouth, 37.0 mm across inside, is as wide as this bar. Mallet: the printed boxwood row, 0.474 m/s, strike 18.2 cm, Table I and Fig. 9. Iowa's bars are not known to be padouk.
